4119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564414 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7d8139c2-5f3f-5d4c-a02c-7713bd2ce796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564428 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7b047d0-d015-53e1-8720-01100b323493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564461 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2d8f279-2cfe-5521-bbd6-ad8903d4bc62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x+y=1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d8749ea-387f-52be-af2d-efee12373f36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564485 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "659cad17-10ef-5a6e-af0d-5c68299c5ad8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564493 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0506b4c-4bb7-5b50-9f2e-88bb9d3c3ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564519 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
